"""
model-space — disk usage across every filesystem, plus tools to move and prune
local LLM model files.

  model-space                  # TUI
  model-space --rescan         # ignore the cached index on startup
  model-space --min-size 1G    # hide models smaller than this (default 200M)
  model-space --root /data     # also scan this folder (repeatable)
  model-space --list           # print the model index and exit (no TUI)

Tabs:  1  Storage    used / free on every disk, and how much of it is models
       2  Move       pick models, pick a destination disk + folder, move them
       3  Clean up   models sorted by last use; select and delete

Keys (tables):  space/enter  select row     a  select all shown    n  clear selection
                /            filter         m  move (tab 2)        d  delete (tab 3)
                click a column header to sort        r  rescan     q  quit

How models are found: every real filesystem is walked (system dirs on / are
skipped) for weight files (.gguf .safetensors .bin .pt .pth .ckpt .onnx ...),
Ollama stores and the Hugging Face hub cache. Multi-part shards are grouped into
one model, and a folder that is mostly weights (a quant folder, an HF snapshot)
is treated as one model so it moves and deletes as a unit.

"Last used" is the newest atime of the model's files. With relatime (Ubuntu's
default) atime is bumped at most once a day when a file is read or mmapped, so
it is accurate to about a day — but anything else that reads the file (a backup,
a checksum) also counts as use.

Moves within one filesystem are a rename. Across filesystems the files are
copied with progress (bypassing the page cache so a running model's cache isn't
evicted), verified, and only then are the originals removed. By default a
symlink is left at the old location so systemd units and scripts that point at
it keep working; existing symlinks to the model are retargeted either way.
Models that a process has open or mmapped are never moved or deleted.
"""

